<TopicPage slug="subjects/geography/foundational/plate-tectonics">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Earth's shell is cracked into a couple of dozen rigid plates riding on slowly flowing hot
    rock. They spread apart at <strong>mid-ocean ridges</strong>, dive under each other at
    <strong>subduction zones</strong>, and grind past one another at <strong>transform
    faults</strong> — moving 2 to 10 cm a year, about the speed your fingernails grow. That
    sounds negligibly slow until you run it for a hundred million years: the Atlantic has
    widened by the width of the Pacific in the time since the dinosaurs' decline. Every
    earthquake, volcano range and mountain chain is the receipt of that motion.
  </p>
  <p>
    The layer cake behind the idea, quickly: the outermost ~100 km is the cold, rigid
    <strong>lithosphere</strong> (crust plus the uppermost mantle), broken into plates; beneath
    it lies the <strong>asthenosphere</strong>, hot solid rock that flows — imperceptibly to us,
    unmistakably over aeons. The plates are not passive rafts on a conveyor: the densest parts
    of them (old, cold ocean floor) sink into the mantle and <em>pull</em> the rest of the plate
    after them, which supplies most of the driving force. And the motion is no longer inferred —
    satellite GPS measures plates sliding at centimetres per year, exactly as the theory
    predicts. A 1960s hypothesis is now a direct measurement.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="from mockery to certainty in fifty years — how a heresy became geology's grand unifier" widget="timeline" config={tectTimeline} />

  <h2>The story of the idea (and why it matters methodologically)</h2>
  <p>
    The timeline above is worth reading as a lesson in how science works. Wegener assembled
    overwhelming evidence in 1912 — matching coastlines, identical fossils on now-distant
    shores, matching rock strata — and was ignored for fifty years because he had no
    <em>mechanism</em>; the physics establishment correctly showed his proposed engine
    (continents ploughing through ocean floor) was impossible. Hess's 1962 "geopoetry" supplied
    the machinery — seafloor spreading at ridges, destruction at trenches — and Vine and
    Matthews' magnetic stripes in 1963 clinched it: the seafloor's alternating magnetic bands,
    symmetrical about every ridge, record Earth's flipping field like a tape recording of the
    spreading itself. By 1968 plate tectonics had unified geology. The portable moral:
    <em>a theory without a mechanism is a story, and evidence without a theory is trivia</em> —
    you need both, and the bitter decade in between is not a scandal but the process.
  </p>

  <h2>Three boundaries, three landscapes</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Divergent boundaries</strong> pull plates apart. Upwelling mantle melts by
    decompression and welds new basaltic crust onto the trailing edges, building the globe-girdling
    mid-ocean ridge system (the longest mountain chain on Earth, mostly unvisited, under water).
    Where rifting starts in a continent you get rift valleys and early volcanism — the East
    African Rift, where Africa is visibly beginning to split; Iceland is a ridge fortuitously
    above sea level, and its fissures, geysers and lava fields are a field trip to the seam of
    the world. <strong>Convergent boundaries</strong> destroy crust. Ocean floor dives
    (<em>subducts</em>) under its neighbour, scraping sediments into trenches — the deepest
    places on Earth — and generating the arc of volcanoes above it: the Andes where an ocean
    plate slides beneath South America, island arcs like Japan and Indonesia where two ocean
    plates collide. Where two continents meet, neither can sink far: the crust thickens and
    buckles into fold mountains — the Himalaya, a 50-million-year collision between India and
    Asia that is still running, still raising Everest about 5 mm a year (erosion fights back,
    roughly to a draw). <strong>Transform boundaries</strong> neither create nor destroy: plates
    slide past each other, as along the San Andreas Fault, producing savage shallow earthquakes
    and little else — no volcanoes, no mountains, just the sticking and snapping of two giants
    in contact.
  </p>

  <h2>Where earthquakes and volcanoes live: the distribution</h2>
  <p>
    Pin every recorded earthquake and volcano on a world map and the randomness dissolves into
    lines — the single most convincing piece of evidence in geology. About three-quarters of the
    world's volcanoes and around 90% of its earthquakes crowd the <strong>Pacific Ring of
    Fire</strong>, which is simply the longest chain of colliding plates. The <strong>mid-ocean
    ridges</strong> form a second, quieter belt (the earthquakes there are small and shallow);
    the <strong>Alpide belt</strong> from the Mediterranean through Turkey, Iran and the
    Himalaya marks the Africa–India–Eurasia collisions; and subduction zones produce the only
    <em>deep</em> earthquakes, descending in inclined planes (Wadati–Benioff zones) to about
    700 km — the subducted slab, quaking as it warms. The exceptions are as informative as the
    pattern: a few volcanoes — Hawaii, Yellowstone — sit far from any edge, over <strong>hot
    spots</strong> where plumes from deep in the mantle melt their way through the plate. Every
    hazard on the syllabus has this same address label: read the map, and the boundary types
    tell you what will happen there.
  </p>

  <h2>Earthquakes: the snap of a strained stick</h2>
  <p>
    An earthquake is <strong>elastic rebound</strong>. Plates push; friction locks the fault;
    rock bends elastically for years or centuries, storing energy like a compressed spring;
    then the fault slips and the stored strain releases in seconds as seismic waves. The point
    of rupture underground is the <em>focus</em>; the point directly above is the
    <em>epicentre</em>. Three wave families race out: <strong>P-waves</strong> (push–pull,
    fastest, arrive first), <strong>S-waves</strong> (side-to-side shear, slower, and unable to
    cross liquid), and the slow, rolling <strong>surface waves</strong> that do most of the
    structural damage. The worldwide seismometer network reads the arrival-time differences to
    locate the focus, and the wave heights to size the event. Magnitude is a logarithmic game:
    each whole step is 10× the ground motion and roughly 32× the energy, so the 2011 Tōhoku,
    Japan earthquake (magnitude 9.1) released about a <em>billion</em> times the energy of a
    modest magnitude 3. Fault types carry the boundary story: normal faults where crust pulls
    apart, reverse (thrust) faults where it compresses, strike-slip where it shears. Under the
    sea, thrust ruptures shove the water and raise <strong>tsunamis</strong> — in 2011, waves
    up to 40 m swept 10 km inland in Japan, killing nearly 20,000. In soft, waterlogged ground,
    shaking can also liquefy soil into quicksand-like slurry, toppling buildings that survived
    the wave itself.
  </p>

  <h2>Volcanoes: two personalities</h2>
  <p>
    Magma has two temperaments, and the difference is chemistry. <strong>Basaltic</strong> magma
    — low in silica, hot, runny — lets gas escape easily, so it erupts gently and flows far,
    building the broad, low <strong>shield volcanoes</strong> of Hawaii, where Kīlauea has
    erupted almost continuously for decades and tourists watch from marked paths.
    <strong>Andesitic-to-rhyolitic</strong> magma — silica-rich, cooler, sticky — traps its gas
    until the pressure is catastrophic, then explodes: this is the magma of subduction zones
    and of the steep, layered <strong>composite (strato-)volcanoes</strong> like Mount St Helens
    (1980, a sideways blast that flattened 600 km² of forest) and Krakatoa (1883, whose bang was
    heard 4,800 km away and whose tsunamis killed over 36,000). The same chemistry explains
    calderas (the collapsed craters left by super-eruptions — Yellowstone's last was 640,000
    years ago and rated the maximum VEI 8), the geysers and hot springs of volcanic terrains,
    and the ash: fine, glassy, and able to circle the planet. Volcanoes even nudge climate —
    Pinatubo's 1991 eruption injected so much sulphate into the stratosphere that global
    temperature fell about half a degree for a year or two, and Tambora's 1815 eruption made
    1816 the "year without a summer", with crop failures across the northern hemisphere.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="the explosive league table — Volcanic Explosivity Index of famous eruptions" widget="chart" config={veiCfg} />

  <p>
    The Volcanic Explosivity Index (VEI) is logarithmic like magnitude: each step is roughly ten
    times the ejected volume. The chart's lesson is the range packed into "volcano" — a
    different word than "eruption" would almost be justified, and the gap between St Helens and
    Tambora (a step of two, a factor of a hundred) is why Tambora caused a famine on another
    continent while St Helens made excellent television.
  </p>

  <h2>Two case studies, one lesson</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Japan, 11 March 2011.</strong> The Pacific plate thrust beneath northern Honshu
    in a magnitude 9.1 rupture — the largest quake in Japan's recorded history, shifting the
    main island 2.4 m east and tilting it slightly upward. Japan's buildings mostly stood:
    the engineered stock behaved, and shaking deaths were a minority of the toll. The ocean
    was the killer — the seafloor rupture launched a tsunami that overtopped 10 m sea walls
    in places, ran up to 40 m on steep coasts, flooded 560 km², killed nearly 20,000 people
    and knocked out the Fukushima power station's cooling. The aftermath rewrote Japanese
    planning: taller gates, rethought coastal defences, nationwide drill culture. <strong>Haiti,
    12 January 2010.</strong> A magnitude 7.0 rupture — a thousand times less energy than
    Tōhoku — struck directly under Port-au-Prince, a dense capital of unreinforced masonry
    with no enforced building code. Over 200,000 people died (estimates run higher), most
    from collapsing buildings. Same planet, same fault mechanics, two decades apart: the
    variable that decided the outcomes was <em>wealth and engineering</em>, not geology.
    Any honest account of tectonic hazard is therefore also an account of development.
  </p>

  <h2>Impacts and management, in brief</h2>
  <p>
    Earthquakes do not kill people; <em>buildings</em> do — which is why the same magnitude 7
    that levelled Port-au-Prince, Haiti in 2010 (over 200,000 dead, by most estimates) is a
    survivable event in Japan, where building codes have two generations of engineering behind
    them. The geography of hazard is really the geography of wealth and preparedness.
    Volcanoes, by contrast, give warning signs that can be monitored — earthquake swarms,
    ground swelling, gas emissions — and monitoring pays: the 1991 Pinatubo evacuation, based
    on such signs, saved tens of thousands of lives. Earthquake <em>prediction</em> in the
    specific sense remains impossible (the system is chaotic; only probabilities can be
    stated), but early-warning networks now exploit the P-wave's head start to send seconds-to-
    a-minute alerts that stop trains and surgery; tsunami warning systems do the same across
    ocean basins. Hazard maps, drills and land-use zoning round out the toolkit — most of the
    battle is administrative, not high-tech.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The theory's frontiers are active. <strong>Seismic tomography</strong> — CAT-scanning the
    mantle with earthquake waves — now images subducted slabs sinking thousands of kilometres
    and plumes rising, letting geologists read the last 200 million years of plate history
    directly. The <strong>supercontinent cycle</strong> extends it: Pangaea assembled around
    300 million years ago and broke up from 175; earlier supercontinents (Rodinia, Columbia)
    came and went on roughly 400–600-million-year rhythms, and extrapolations suggest a
    "Pangaea Proxima" a couple of hundred million years ahead. Whether plate tectonics is a
    one-planet peculiarity is a serious question: Venus lacks it (its crust seems to recycle
    in episodic global overplating), Mars froze out of it aeons ago, and some argue Earth's
    water is the essential lubricant — which would make plate tectonics another entry on the
    shortlist of things that make this planet habitable. The deepest lesson, though, stays
    local: the ground beneath your feet is a machine in motion, and the map of its joints is
    the most complete safety notice humanity has ever been handed.
  </p>
